  "query_preview": "..Water vapor shows a trough over the central and\nnorthern Great Plains and a ridge building into the northern\nGreat Basin and Pacific Northwest. The ridge will help pinch \noff a cutoff low later today that will sit over southern \nCalifornia and northern Baja through Saturday. At the surface \nhigh pressure is beginning to form over Montana and Wyoming and \nwill move south along the lee of the Rockies and strengthen over\nthe next 24 hours. By Friday afternoon the center of the high \nwill be over eastern Colorado and will be 3.5-4.5 standard \ndeviations above average. This will set up a very strong \npressure gradient over southern Arizona leading to a prolonged \nvery strong easterly wind event. \n\nNAEFS mean 850 mb winds are 40-55 kts Friday morning into the\nafternoon which is 5-7 standard deviations above the CFSR\nclimatology. The ECMWF EFI for wind speed is showing widespread\nvalues of 0.95-1.0 which signifies a very anomalous wind event.\nAlso, thanks to the position of the closed low over Baja \n700-500 mb winds will be out of the southeast, so momentum \ntransfer down to the surface will have a similar direction to \nthe surface, which is not typical for east wind events. \n\nHi-res models all indicate that the strongest winds will be on \nthe leeward side (west to northwest side) of mountain ranges \nindicating that wind gusts will be aided by mountain wave action\nas well as downsloping. REFS wind gust probabilities for 70 mph\nor greater in the lee of the mountains is 50-90 percent Friday \nmorning and early afternoon and again Friday night. It's even \nshowing localized areas on the lee side of the Chiricahuas and \nnorthwest of the Catalinas (i.e. Saddlebrooke) that have a \n20-50 percent chance to see 80 mph gusts Friday morning. \nElsewhere expect sustained speeds 30-45 mph with gusts 50-70 \nmph. These winds will be capable of downing trees and power \npoles as well as generating blowing dust. High profile vehicles \nshould also use caution when driving in cross winds. \n\nThe High Wind Warning was extended 6 hours to 5 AM Saturday due\nto new model guidance showing HWW criteria sustained winds and \ngusts lasting past the initial end time of 11 PM Friday. A Wind\nAdvisory is then in effect from 5 AM Saturday through 2 PM\nSaturday.\n\nIn the tropics Simon is about 270 miles southeast of Cabo and\nwill travel northwest paralleling the Cabo coast this weekend\ninto early next week. It's track is trending further west over\nthe past 24 hours. Even with the more westward track, the closed\nlow over Baja will help transport moisture from Simon into\nsoutheast Arizona Friday into next week. Friday western Pima\nCounty will see a slight chance for showers and storms, but HREF\nand REFS members keep most of the activity in Sonora. Saturday\nchances increase for most of southeast Arizona with 20-50 PoPs.\nSunday could see a brief break in rain chances, but the upper \nair pattern stays fairly progressive. By Sunday the closed low \nover Baja opens up and flattens out as a Pacific low approaches \nthe Pacific Northwest and then moves down the west coast early \nto mid next week. Moisture sticks around and this will lead to \ncontinued below normal temperatures and 20-50 PoPs through mid \nnext week.",
